# modules/dast/checks.rules - the script-check registry for the §7.4 phases that
# live at the TOP LEVEL of modules/dast/ (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5;
# docs/STEP5-DAST-PLAN.md tier 5).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT THIS PATH, AND WHY IT IS SHARED.
# rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's path table gives the §9.5 script-check schema to
# "any file named `checks.rules`, at any depth", and makes every other record
# path E070 - so a per-script `authz-checks.rules` is not a legal record file,
# however tidy it would look while five tier-5 tickets are built in parallel.
# modules/dast/passive/ and modules/dast/active/ each already have one, and this
# is the third: the home for jwt.sh, graphql.sh, ratelimit.sh, authz.sh and
# transport.sh, whose scripts sit at this level rather than in a sub-directory.
# TREAT IT AS APPEND-ONLY.  A merge conflict in it is resolved by keeping BOTH
# blocks, never by choosing a side, exactly as passive/checks.rules records for
# its own peers.
#
# THE TYPE TAG IS `active`, matching the phase table's `authz.sh:active` floor
# (modules/dast/engine.sh), so these checks are reachable only at
# `--intensity active`.  They are NOT tagged `intrusive`: every request is a
# read-only GET or HEAD of a reference the target itself published, nothing is
# created, modified or deleted, and requiring --allow-intrusive on top of the
# active-intensity opt-in would be a second, undocumented gate.
#
# `requires-identities: 2` is the key rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5 froze with
# `authz.sh` named in its own row.  Object-level authorization cannot be
# established from one session, so a run with fewer than two AUTHENTICATED
# identities skips these checks with a stated reason rather than running them
# against one identity and reporting the result.
#
# FOUR IDS RATHER THAN ONE, because the DAST location profile is (target,
# method, path_template, param_location, param_name) and carries nothing that
# names the DEFECT: a confirmed cross-user read and an over-exposed field on the
# same endpoint would otherwise hash to one fingerprint and findings_merge would
# silently keep whichever sorted first.  IDOR and CROSS_IDENTITY_READ are split
# for the same reason and additionally because they are different CLAIMS - see
# modules/dast/authz_engine.sh's header for the oracle that separates them.

id: DAST-AUTHZ-IDOR-01
title: Object reference is readable by an identity that does not own it
script: authz.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-639
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Authorize every request against the object it names, not merely
  against the session. Load the object, then check that the authenticated
  principal is entitled to it, in the same server-side code path for every
  handler - a check performed in the client, in a route guard, or only on the
  listing endpoint is not one. Making the identifier unguessable (a UUID
  instead of a counter) narrows discovery but is not the fix: this finding is
  evidence that a reference obtained by any means is honoured for the wrong
  principal.
references: CWE-639
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-AUTHZ-CROSS_IDENTITY_READ-01
title: Two separate identities receive the identical non-public object
script: authz.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-639
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Confirm whether this object is intended to be shared between all
  authenticated users. If it is, no change is needed and this finding can be
  baselined. If it is not, authorize the request against the object rather than
  against the session, exactly as for a confirmed insecure direct object
  reference. This check reports an observation rather than asserting a defect
  because, unlike DAST-AUTHZ-IDOR-01, it saw no evidence anywhere on this
  endpoint that per-object ownership is enforced at all.
references: CWE-639
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/

id: DAST-AUTHZ-EXCESSIVE_DATA-01
title: Authenticated response carries fields beyond what the view needs
script: authz.sh
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-213
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Serialise a response from an explicit view model naming the fields
  the client needs, rather than returning a database row or a domain object and
  relying on the client to ignore the rest. A credential hash, an API token, a
  privilege flag or a regulated personal-data field that reaches the browser is
  disclosed to everything that can read that response - the page itself, an
  injected script, an intermediary log, and the browser cache - whether or not
  the interface renders it. The field list this check matches against is data,
  in modules/dast/sensitive-fields.txt.
references: CWE-213
references: https://owasp.org/API-Security/editions/2023/en/0xa3-broken-object-property-level-authorization/

id: DAST-AUTHZ-OTHER_IDENTITY_DATA-01
title: Authenticated response contains another identity's identifier
script: authz.sh
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-200
owasp: A01:2021
tags: active
coverage-scope: target
requires-config: config/auth.conf
requires-identities: 2
remediation: Scope every query to the authenticated principal where the data is
  fetched, not where it is rendered. A response carrying a second user
  identifier usually means a listing or a join returned every row and the
  filtering was left to the presentation layer, so the whole table is available
  to anyone who reads the response body directly.
references: CWE-200
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A01_2021-Broken_Access_Control/
